\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The evolution of a population of candidates includes all common evolutionary
 methods and is divided by default into 10% reproduction, 55% mutation and
 35% crossover in all runs.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "tab:Distribution-gp-evolutions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Olymp-reproduction (Pareto reproduction)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similar to the Greek gods who came to earth to reproduce, the pareto candidates
 were also given the opportunity to intermingle with the current population.
 By chance, solutions already found and their genes could be completely
 lost.
 In order not to lose already found solutions permanently, the genes of
 the best candidates are to provide a very small current for backups of
 the best-ofs of the past.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The olymp reproduction includes in particular the possibility for the origin
 tree to reproduce itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Fixed nodes
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:depth-based-gp-implementation"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fixed nodes can be determined manually for a real subset of nodes of the
 origin tree and are unaffected by the evolution process.
 In order to continue to use classical GP evolution with the same intuition,
 some procedures, especially when generating random trees, must therefore
 be adapted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fixed core of a tree must be contiguous in this implementation, but
 can offer starting points for evolutions at any number of points at different
 heights.
 The node depth, which normally indicates the distance to the tree root,
 thus almost completely loses its meaning for the GP process.
 The depth of a node is therefore replaced with a shell-based interpretation,
 in which the distance of a node to the next fixed node is used as.
 Due to the high branching factor in trees with several GP interfaces, however,
 the layer model also loses its function, which is why the number of nodes
 replaces the tree depth as a complexity limit and complexity target in
 random tree generation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename img/inkscape/fix_nodes_mutation.pdf
	rotateOrigin center

\end_inset


\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Fixed nodes must not be changed during the evolve process.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Nodesize-based build options including the full- and grow-method.
 Randomly generated trees should cover the entire search space and have
 the structuring Full/Grow options to have the same properties of non-fixed
 generated trees.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The maximum tree depth is a common factor for random , the generation of
 random trees and branches is usually based on the tree depth.
 However, this is a problem for the layerwise mutation which is needed for
 example with fixed nodes, where several branches have to be built up at
 the same time.
 As the tree size can grow exponentially, the starting depth may differ
 between branches and the trees may end up very unbalanced.
 Therefore a node based algorithm was developed, which builds up branches
 in full- and grow mode up to a certain number of nodes without risk of
 structural imbalances.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order not to violate the principles of GP, some factors must be considered.
 At the beginning a random number of nodes to be inserted is chosen.
 Instead of the node depth, layers are defined, which are the distance to
 the next fixed node.
 Depending on the number of nodes located on a layer, the number of nodes
 to be inserted is randomly divided into intervals.
 These subtrees are divided according to the Grow or Full method up to the
 interval size they are entitled to.
 The number of nodes must be exactly reached (tolerance -1), otherwise the
 trees could have a large variance of nodes per branch.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When building the branches, a buffer of leaf nodes is built up to avoid
 having to prune the tree at the end.
 To build one level lower, a random node of the ebend is selected and assigned
 according to the Grow or Full method.
 This avoids an overload of the left side, which would occur because only
 limited operators can be used.
\end_layout

